# Clickjacking is decided by `frame-ancestors`, not by X-Frame-Options. CSP
# Level 2 requires a browser to ignore X-Frame-Options on any response that
# also carries a frame-ancestors directive, and every layer that sends a policy
# here sends one. So while these two lines said `frame-ancestors 'self'` and
# `X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN`, and netlify.toml said `X-Frame-Options: DENY`,
# what every current browser actually enforced was 'self' from all of them —
# same-origin framing allowed. Settling that disagreement by flipping only the
# X-Frame-Options strings to DENY would have left that unchanged.
#
# 'none' is the honest value. Nothing frames an ATHENA page: every <iframe>
# under src/ embeds a third party (YouTube and Vimeo in the classroom page,
# Stripe in the payment form), which is `frame-src`'s business, and both
# markdown sanitisers strip iframes out of member content. X-Frame-Options
# still says DENY for browsers too old to understand frame-ancestors.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; media-src 'self' blob: https:; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()
  X-XSS-Protection: 1; mode=block
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
